15 0 obj
<< /A 40 0 R /Alt (Flowchart illustrating patient eligibility and data processing for a study, starting with 963 screened patients, showing exclusion criteria and data loss, leading to final analysis of 610 patients, then random grouping into training and validation cohorts, model construction using logistic regression and nomogram, followed by model validation via ROC curve, Hosmer-Lemeshow test, and Decision Curve Analysis, concluding with model establishment indicating practical applicability.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 29 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
16 0 obj
<< /A 41 0 R /Alt (Forest plot and corresponding table presenting odds ratios with ninety-five percent confidence intervals and p-values for factors including gender, FS-14, N3 percent, retirement, and various shift types; 8-hour, 12-hour, and 24-hour shifts all show significantly increased odds with wide confidence intervals and p-values less than 0.001.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 34 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
17 0 obj
<< /A 42 0 R /Alt (Nomogram graphic with predictor variables including work schedules, FS-14, SWS\(N3%\), and gender, each assigned specific points summed to yield a total of approximately 208, predicting a probability outcome of about zero point nine.) /K 1 /P 8 0 R /Pg 34 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
18 0 obj
<< /A 43 0 R /Alt (Panel A shows a ROC curve for a training set with an AUC of 0.808, sensitivity of 0.846, and specificity of 0.667. Panel B displays a ROC curve for a validation set with an AUC of 0.767, sensitivity of 0.7, and specificity of 0.777. Both graphs plot sensitivity versus one minus specificity, with dashed reference lines for comparison.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 35 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
19 0 obj
<< /A 44 0 R /Alt (Panel A presents a calibration plot comparing predicted and actual probabilities for Pr{outcome=1}, featuring dotted, solid, and dashed lines for apparent, bias-corrected, and ideal predictions; mean absolute error is 0.016, n equals 427. Panel B displays a similar plot for validation data with a mean absolute error of 0.036, n equals 183, and includes the same three reference lines.) /K 1 /P 8 0 R /Pg 35 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
20 0 obj
<< /A 45 0 R /Alt (Two line graphs labeled A and B compare standardized net benefit versus high risk threshold and cost:benefit ratio, with three lines per graph representing a nomogram model, all, and none. Both graphs show the nomogram model, in red, providing higher standardized net benefit across most thresholds compared to the all and none strategies. A legend in the top right of each graph identifies the lines, and both plots share the same axes and scaling for direct comparison.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 36 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
